## 4. Magnetic-driven winds and X-ray emission

Defining the conversion efficiency of EM luminosity into observed X-ray luminosity as \\eta\\equiv\\,L_{\\mbox{\\tiny EM}}^{\\mbox{\\tiny obs}}/L_{\\mbox{\\tiny EM}}, we can use the results of our simulations for characteristic radii and periods (R_{e}\\sim 10^{6}\\,\\mathrm{cm} and P\\sim 10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{s}) to deduce that magnetic field strengths in the range \\bar{B}\\sim\\eta^{-1/2}\\times 10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{-}\\,10^{16}\\,\\mathrm{G} are needed to produce luminosities L_{\\mbox{\\tiny EMS}}^{\\mbox{\\tiny obs}}\\sim 10^{46}\\,\\mathrm{-}\\,10^{51}\\, \\mathrm{erg\\,s^{-1}}, which characterize the extended emission and X-ray plateaus of SGRBs (e.g., Rowlinson et al., 2013; Gompertz et al., 2014). Assuming an efficiency of, e.g., \\eta\\sim 0.01\\,\\mathrm{-}\\,0.1 yields magnetic field strengths \\bar{B}\\sim 10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{-}\\,10^{17}\\,\\mathrm{G}. These strengths are not reached in the progenitor neutron stars, but they can be built from much weaker initial magnetic fields in a number of ways: the compression of stellar cores (Giacomazzo

